All videos are 1280 × 720, 16:9, H.264, yuv420p, constant 60 fps, silent, with fast-start metadata. There are no captions, logos, loading bars, gameplay or interactive elements. These are rendered video concepts, not Unity prefabs or engine performance tests.

## Timing and use

The preview demonstrates three joined phases:

| Time | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| 0.00–2.50 s | Dim wick develops into a restrained flame; internal light reaches the hold state. |
| 2.50–6.50 s | Four-second breathing hold. Flame, light, optical reflections, dust and embers are periodic. |
| 6.50–7.65 s | Completion rises smoothly from the hold state to full brightness. |
| 7.65–8.00 s | Full light settles; flame motion continues. |

The hold is approximately 38% of the completion control, so it does not announce completion early. For an unspecified loading duration, play the intro once, repeat the hold, then play complete. The separate files join at the periodic boundary. The supplied completion MP4 starts at hold phase zero; waiting for that exact boundary can add up to four seconds before completion. It is not an arbitrary-phase video transition. For immediate source-level completion without resetting motion, call `frame("complete", elapsed, phase_offset=incoming_hold_phase_seconds)`: light uses completion elapsed while the motion continues from the incoming phase. Integration is not demonstrated or certified. For a fixed-length screen, use the complete eight-second preview as provided. No duplicated endpoint frame is included in the hold.

## What is editable

`source/Lantern_Ignition.blend` is the original Blender 4.3.2 prop, camera, physically lit set and procedural materials. It contains modeled iron frame, worn brass inlays, rivets, open chimney, carrying bail and chain. No external textures are required.

`source/build_lantern.py` rebuilds that scene and renders two original RGBA lighting plates, unlit and internally lit. `source/plates/projection.json` records camera-space anchors and the front pane. Included plates allow animation to be rebuilt without rendering Blender again.

`source/animate.py` is the actual motion source. This is a hybrid plate/compositor animation, not a fully animated 3D simulation. It computes the flame contour, hotter core, secondary tongue, correlated internal lighting, glass reflections, fine smoke, rising embers, floating dust and tiny suspended sway independently at every 1/60-second sample. The flame is an analytical moving field, not a still-image zoom or frame-rate conversion. The supplied Blender scene is the lighting/geometry source; the video motion is generated by this Python compositor.

## Rebuild

Install Python 3 with the packages in `source/requirements.txt`; have `ffmpeg` on PATH.

From the extracted folder:

```sh
python source/animate.py
```

To rebuild a particular clip:

```sh
python source/animate.py --mode preview
python source/animate.py --mode hold
```

To rebuild the optional lighting plates with Blender 4.3:

```sh
blender -b -t 4 --python source/build_lantern.py
```

Then run the animation compositor again. Plate rendering uses CPU Cycles, 128 samples, without requiring a denoiser. Lighting, palette, camera, iron geometry and material wear are in `build_lantern.py`. The completion plate includes a deliberately art-directed warm return-light rig so the outer hood and lower shell reveal their detail. The procedural flame and this rig share the same smooth intensity control. Motion, timing and brightness are in `animate.py`, especially `time_state()` and `frame()`.

## Art direction

Original artwork with an austere, mature action-fantasy atmosphere. The reference to Diablo II / Path of Exile 2 was used only as a tonal direction; no extracted franchise artwork, assets, logos or audio are included. This package contains no licensed third-party visual assets.
